<h2 class="ds-section-title">How it works</h2>

<div class="ds-callout">
  <p class="ds-intro-text">
    DB Diff cuts a table into segments of primary keys and checksums each one on both sides. Segments whose checksums
    agree are skipped without reading a row. A segment that differs is read row by row, so the cost follows the number
    of differing segments rather than the size of the table.
  </p>
  <p class="ds-intro-text">
    Each column is hashed on its own and the digests are folded together, which is what removes the ambiguity of joining
    values before hashing them. A NULL bitmap sits in front, because a NULL and an empty string render alike and a
    column that is one on each side would otherwise look equal.
  </p>
  <p class="ds-intro-text">
    Within one engine the checksum hashes the engine's own rendering of each value. Across engines that is not enough:
    PostgreSQL writes a decimal as <code>1.50</code> and ClickHouse writes the same number as <code>1.5</code>, and a
    difference in spelling would read as a difference in data. Pro reduces every value to a canonical form first, so
    what gets hashed is the value rather than the notation.
  </p>

  <div class="ds-ctas">
    <a
      href="https://github.com/clubmatto/vetrina/blob/main/db-diff/docs/how-it-works.md"
      class="ds-btn ds-btn--primary"
      >Read the algorithm notes</a
    >
  </div>
</div>

<h2 class="ds-section-title">DB Diff CLI</h2>

<div class="ds-callout">
  <p class="ds-intro-text">
    Prefer working from the command line? <strong>DB Diff</strong> is a single binary with no server and no state. It
    exits <code>0</code> when the tables match, <code>1</code> when they differ, and <code>2</code> when it could not
    run, so a job can tell "the tables differ" apart from "the tool broke".
  </p>

  <div class="ds-ctas">
    <a href="/vetrina/db-diff/" class="ds-btn ds-btn--primary">Try the CLI</a>
  </div>
</div>

